Subject: [PATCH] Sell what was built: README, a cold-start CI job, and no
 image per push
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three defects found by auditing this repository as a stranger would see it.
None of them is in the product, and all of them are expensive.

**The README sold the wrong thing.** The front page — the first and often only
thing a prospect, a partner or an investor reads — said "NeuralBridge is a
lightweight integration hub for AI workflows" and contained zero occurrences of
assurance, the Cyber Resilience Act, the Machinery Regulation or Article 14.
Twelve patches of product were invisible on their own front page.

It now leads with the problem (paperwork that quietly stops describing the
machine), the two regulations that turned that into a legal one with their
dates, and the one command a plant engineer runs. NeuralBridge is kept below,
described honestly as the platform this grew out of. Every row of the
capability table maps to a command that runs, and every CLI verb in it was
checked against --help before it was written down: the same rule as the code,
which is that a claim nothing enforces is a claim nobody should make.

**A container image was published on every push to main.** publish-docker
triggered on `push: branches: [main]`, so every commit spent runner minutes
publishing unfinished software to GHCR and gave anybody reading that registry a
version number that means nothing. Images are now cut from releases and version
tags only.

**Nothing proved the product works from a cold start.** The unit suite runs
inside a tree that is already installed, configured and warm — 671 tests that
say nothing about whether a stranger can use this. assurance.yml starts from a
bare checkout on 3.11, 3.12 and 3.13, installs the way the README tells a
stranger to install, checks every CLI domain starts, then runs the offline
enrolment kit end to end: init, fill, check, run. It asserts the verdict is
complete, the airgap held, the report carries its own limits, and the
attestation request carries a real head hash.

Then it does the part that matters most. It has a second party counter-sign the
head, deletes records from the ledger, confirms the chain still verifies —
which is the whole problem — and fails the build if the rewind is not detected.
If the central claim of this product ever stops being true, CI goes red on the
commit that broke it rather than on the day a customer finds out.

Every step of that workflow was executed locally before it was committed. A
workflow that has never run is a guess.

